1. First verify that the metrics exist

Start by querying the Prometheus endpoint from the openshift-monitoring namespace:

oc -n openshift-monitoring exec prometheus-k8s-0 -- \
  curl -s 'http://localhost:9090/api/v1/query?query=vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress' \
  | jq .

Or get only the useful labels and values:

oc -n openshift-monitoring exec prometheus-k8s-0 -- \
  curl -sG 'http://localhost:9090/api/v1/query' \
  --data-urlencode 'query=vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress' \
  | jq -r '.data.result[] |
    [.metric.owner_uid, .metric.pod, .metric.namespace, .value[1]] |
    @tsv'

You should get something similar to:

PVC_UID                              POD                         NAMESPACE       PROGRESS
abc123...                            xcopy-populator-xxxxx       openshift-mtv  37
def456...                            xcopy-populator-yyyyy       openshift-mtv  82

If this returns nothing, don't proceed with the queries yet. First determine whether the MTV metrics are actually being scraped.

2. Find the PVC UID

The AI-generated solution refers to:

owner_uid="<pvc-uid>"

So first identify the PVC associated with the disk being migrated.

For example:

oc get pvc -A

Then:

oc get pvc <PVC_NAME> -n <NAMESPACE> \
  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.uid}{"\n"}'

Example:

oc get pvc winvm-disk-0 -n openshift-mtv \
  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.uid}{"\n"}'

Output:

8c4f2d5a-....

Now use that UID in Prometheus.

3. Check disk-transfer progress

Run:

vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{
  owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
}

For example:

vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{
  owner_uid="8c4f2d5a-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx"
}

You might get:

42

That means approximately 42% of the disk transfer has completed.

You can also query directly from OpenShift:

oc -n openshift-monitoring exec prometheus-k8s-0 -- \
  curl -sG 'http://localhost:9090/api/v1/query' \
  --data-urlencode \
  'query=vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{owner_uid="8c4f2d5a-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx"}' \
  | jq -r '.data.result[] | .value[1]'
4. Calculate ETA

Once you have progress, check whether progress is actually increasing.

Use:

deriv(
  vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{
    owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
  }[5m]
)

Suppose:

deriv = 0.20

That means approximately:

0.20 percentage points/second

If current progress is 40%:

remaining = 100 - 40
          = 60%

ETA:

60 / 0.20 = 300 seconds

So approximately:

5 minutes remaining

The complete PromQL expression is:

(
  100 -
  vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{
    owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
  }
)
/
deriv(
  vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{
    owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
  }[5m]
)
Important

Don't use the ETA blindly.

If:

deriv(...) <= 0

then the transfer is not progressing, and the ETA is either meaningless or infinite.

So I'd actually use:

(
  100 - vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"}
)
/
deriv(
  vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"}[5m]
)

only when:

deriv(
  vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"}[5m]
) > 0
5. Detect whether XCOPY/offload was used

Check:

vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_xcopy_used{
  owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
}

You may see:

1

or:

0

Typically:

Value	Interpretation
1	XCOPY/offload was used
0	XCOPY was not used; software-copy path/fallback

So you can query all affected disks:

vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_xcopy_used == 0

However, be careful with this metric while the migration is still running. A value of 0 isn't necessarily proof that the migration has failed. You should correlate it with the migration state and terminal status.

6. Check whether the migration actually failed

First look at the MTV migration resources.

For example:

oc get migration -A

Depending on your MTV version/API, you can inspect the migration:

oc get migration <MIGRATION_NAME> -n <NAMESPACE> -o yaml

You can also check:

oc get plan -A

and:

oc get vm.forklift.konveyor.io -A

For Prometheus, check:

ALERTS{
  alertname="MigrationFailed",
  alertstate="firing"
}

You can make the result easier to read:

oc -n openshift-monitoring exec prometheus-k8s-0 -- \
  curl -sG 'http://localhost:9090/api/v1/query' \
  --data-urlencode 'query=ALERTS{alertname="MigrationFailed",alertstate="firing"}' \
  | jq -r '.data.result[] |
    [.metric.plan_name,
     .metric.phase,
     .metric.mode,
     .metric.provider,
     .metric.status,
     .value[1]] |
    @tsv'

This is useful because you can get information such as:

PLAN             PHASE           MODE       PROVIDER    STATUS
vmware-to-ocp    CopyDisks       live       vmware      Failed
7. Detect a stuck disk transfer

This is one of the most useful queries for your runbook.

Run:

deriv(
  vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{
    owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
  }[15m]
) == 0

But I'd recommend adding the progress condition:

deriv(
  vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{
    owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
  }[15m]
) == 0
and
vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress{
  owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
} < 100

This means:

The transfer has not progressed during the last 15 minutes and has not completed.

Better production alert

Create an alerting rule:

groups:
- name: mtv-storage-offload
  rules:

  - alert: MTVXCopyTransferStuck
    expr: |
      deriv(
        vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress[15m]
      ) == 0
      and
      vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress < 100
    for: 10m
    labels:
      severity: warning
    annotations:
      summary: "MTV XCOPY volume transfer appears stuck"
      description: "XCOPY volume transfer for PVC owner {{ $labels.owner_uid }} has not progressed for 25 minutes."

Why 25 minutes?

15 minutes = measurement window
10 minutes = alert 'for' duration

This reduces false positives from temporary metric/reporting gaps.

8. Check source disk size

First verify that this metric exists:

vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_source_disk_bytes

For a specific PVC:

vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_source_disk_bytes{
  owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
}

Convert to GiB:

vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_source_disk_bytes{
  owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
} / 1024 / 1024 / 1024

For example:

2147483648000 bytes

would be approximately:

2000 GiB
9. Calculate effective transfer throughput

If your duration metric is available:

mtv_vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_copy_duration_seconds{
  owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
}

then:

vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_source_disk_bytes{
  owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
}
/
mtv_vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_copy_duration_seconds{
  owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
}

gives bytes/sec.

Convert to MiB/sec:

(
  vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_source_disk_bytes{
    owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
  }
  /
  mtv_vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_copy_duration_seconds{
    owner_uid="<PVC_UID>"
  }
)
/1024/1024

For example:

2 TB / 10,000 sec

approximately:

200 MB/s

This is much more useful than looking at copy duration alone.

10. Correlate PVC → VM → Migration → Plan

This is the most important part of the AI-generated answer.

Prometheus probably won't directly give you:

Plan
  ↓
VM
  ↓
PVC
  ↓
XCOPY transfer

with all attributes in one metric.

You therefore need to establish the relationship.

A useful troubleshooting flow is:

MTV Plan
   │
   ├── VM
   │
   └── Migration
          │
          └── VM migration status
                   │
                   └── PVC
                          │
                          └── owner_uid
                                 │
                                 └── XCOPY metrics

Start with:

oc get plan -A

Then:

oc get migration -A

Then inspect the migration:

oc get migration <MIGRATION_NAME> \
  -n <NAMESPACE> -o yaml

Then identify the VM:

oc get vm -A

And inspect the VM migration status:

oc get vm.forklift.konveyor.io <VM_NAME> \
  -n <NAMESPACE> -o yaml
11. Check phase-by-phase VM migration status

The command from your generated solution is useful:

oc get vm.forklift.konveyor.io <VM_NAME> \
  -n <PLAN_NAMESPACE> \
  -o jsonpath='{.status.migration.vms[0].pipeline}'

For easier troubleshooting, I'd initially use:

oc get vm.forklift.konveyor.io <VM_NAME> \
  -n <NAMESPACE> -o yaml

and locate:

status:
  migration:
    vms:

You should inspect the pipeline entries for information such as:

step name
phase
progress
completed
total
started
completed

This allows you to determine something like:

VM: Windows-VM01
CPU: 16
Memory: 32 GiB
Disk: 2 TiB

Pipeline:

DiskAllocation     Completed
DiskTransfer       Running       63%
ConvertGuest        Pending
PowerOn             Pending

That's considerably more useful for an RCA than simply saying:

Migration is running
12. Recommended Prometheus dashboard

For the migration monitoring you're building, I'd create the dashboard around these panels:

Panel	PromQL / Data	Purpose
Disk transfer progress	vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_progress	Current %
Transfer ETA	(100-progress)/deriv(progress[5m])	Estimated remaining time
Transfer throughput	source_disk_bytes / copy_duration_seconds	Effective throughput
XCOPY used	vsphere_xcopy_volume_populator_xcopy_used	Offload vs fallback
Stuck transfer	deriv(progress[15m]) == 0	Detect stalled transfer
Migration failed	ALERTS{alertname="MigrationFailed"}	Detect failure
Source disk size	source_disk_bytes	Disk being transferred
VM migration phase	MTV VM status	Current migration stage
13. For your 2-TB / 16-vCPU / 32-GiB VM

I'd structure the monitoring output approximately like this:

VM:        VM01
Plan:      VMware-to-OCP
CPU:       16 vCPU
Memory:    32 GiB
